\subsection{Aim 2, model complexity: when does transfer help?}
\label{sec:aim2}

Whether the source helps at all is judged against the baseline, each regressor fit
on the target data alone. On the aligned lumped target (TBCM, the same family as
the BCM source), the source is already a strong prior, by itself reaching an $F_0$
$R^2$ of @@TBCM_SOURCE_F0@@ with no target data, and optimized transfer clears the
baseline, raising the $F_0$ $R^2$ from @@TBCM_BASE_F0@@ to @@TBCM_TRANS_F0@@ at
$N=50$. On the far continuum target (BM), the same source reaches an $F_0$ $R^2$
of @@BM_SOURCE_F0@@, less accurate than guessing each output's average value, and
transfer does not clear the baseline at all: the best target-alone family reaches
an $F_0$ $R^2$ of @@BM_BASE_F0_3@@ and the best transfer variant
@@BM_TRANS_F0_3@@, a difference of @@BM_F0_DELTA@@. The source is not merely
unhelpful here, it is dead weight. Transfer's
payoff therefore tracks how close the source is to the target, exactly the
principle it rests on.

TabPFN, which uses no source, is the strongest method on BM from
$N=@@BM_TABPFN_FROM_N@@$ upward and on TBCM from $N=@@TBCM_TABPFN_FROM_N@@$
upward, with an $F_0$ $R^2$ of @@TBCM_TABPFN_F0@@ on TBCM and @@BM_TABPFN_F0@@ on
BM at $N=50$. It is not uniformly best: on the aligned target at the smallest
sample sizes, optimized transfer is clearly ahead of it
(@@TBCM_TRANS_F0_N10@@ versus @@TBCM_TABPFN_F0_N10@@ at $N=10$). That exception is
not a counterexample to the principle but an instance of it. Transfer wins
exactly where a well-aligned source is available and target data is scarcest,
which is the regime transfer was designed for; the moment the source stops being a
good prior, as on BM, that advantage disappears entirely. What the no-source model
buys is not universal superiority but insensitivity to the fidelity gap. Its
worst shortfall against the best method in any target-and-sample-size combination
we tested is @@TABPFN_WORST_DEFICIT@@ $R^2$, and it occurs on the aligned target
where a good source exists; the transfer family's worst shortfall is
@@TRANSFER_WORST_DEFICIT@@ $R^2$, and it occurs on the far target where no good
source is available and none can be substituted.

\input{table2_fidelity.tex}

The practical form of this result is not how much the source adds, but whether
optimized transfer is the method to reach for at all. On the aligned target it
plainly is: averaged over outputs and the small sample sizes ($N \le 50$) the
paper is about, optimized transfer ranks @@TBCM_TRANS_RANK_LO@@ among the seven
configurations, and it beats the no-source model in @@TBCM_TRANS_WINS_LO@@ of
those cells. On the far target it plainly is not. Its mean rank never improves
on @@BM_TRANS_RANK_BEST@@ at any sample size, it beats the no-source model in
only @@BM_TRANS_WINS_ALL@@ cells across the whole grid, and its gain over the
same regressor fit on the target alone is @@BM_TRANS_GAIN_N50_3@@ at $N=50$.
The same procedure, the same code and the same seeds thus produce the best
available method on one target and never the best on the other. What separates
the two cases is how close the source model is to the target, which makes
source--target proximity a useful guide to whether a transfer pipeline is worth
building for a given target.

Beyond point accuracy, a useful surrogate must reconstruct the physiological
structure of the $(a_{CT} \times a_{TA})$ control space. On TBCM, which carries a
dense ODE ground-truth map on the complete $40 \times 40$ grid, TabPFN
reconstructs the full motor-control surface to an image-level $R^2$ above 0.9
from $N=@@TBCM_MAP_N@@$ samples and converges to the ground truth by $N=1000$. It
has lower image-level error than optimized transfer at @@TBCM_MAP_WINS@@
(Fig.~\ref{fig:aim2}); below that, transfer reconstructs the surface better,
mirroring the point-accuracy ordering on this aligned target. On BM the
separation is unambiguous: against a dense full-data reference TabPFN has the
lower image-level error at @@BM_MAP_WINS@@, reaching an image-level $R^2$ above
0.9 by $N \approx @@BM_MAP_N@@$, while the transfer surface stays blocky and
dragged toward the misaligned source and never separates from the target-alone
baseline.

