\subsection{Aim 3, richer physiological outputs}
\label{sec:aim3}

$F_0$ and SPL are the conventional surrogate targets, but clinical use turns on
quantities that describe tissue loading and voice quality. We therefore extend
the BM target with three further outputs: AC glottal flow (ACFL), peak collision
pressure (PC), and cepstral peak prominence (CPP). The BM dataset was regenerated
row-aligned to the original 5000 operating points so that these outputs
correspond to exactly the same simulations as the existing $F_0$ and SPL values.

The ordering of methods is unchanged on the richer outputs: at $N=50$ TabPFN
leads on @@AIM3_TABPFN_WINS@@ of the five outputs, and the mean transfer gain over
the target-alone baseline across ACFL, PC and CPP is @@AIM3_GAIN@@ $R^2$
(Table~\ref{tab:aim1}). What does change is the difficulty ranking. ACFL is the
easiest output for every method (TabPFN $R^2 = @@BM_TABPFN_ACFL@@$ at $N=50$),
while CPP is by a clear margin the hardest (TabPFN $R^2 = @@BM_TABPFN_CPP@@$),
with PC intermediate ($R^2 = @@BM_TABPFN_PC@@$). CPP is a cepstral voice-quality
measure computed from the radiated pressure waveform rather than a direct
mechanical quantity, and it is the output least determined by the three input
activations alone, which is the most likely reason no method reaches high
accuracy on it at any $N$ we tested. This is a limit of the input
parameterisation rather than of any one regressor.

